{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_fvy6tiwc72gwo3hh7a7uuoeu7ml2qokm6omt2toro6cyqhvgjwuq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, and the work found **three real divergences** — none of which the existing happy-path behavior revealed.\n\n## Setup\n\nThe C project is an **executable** (`add_executable(driver src/main.c)`), which exports no dynamic symbols, so I additionally built the same translation unit as a `.so` and gave the Rust crate a `cdylib` target with `#[no_mangle]` wrappers. Tests drive both channels: subprocess exe-vs-exe, and `libloading` `.so`-vs-`.so` (in-process, plus a hermetic `so_runner` example for anything touching stdin).\n\n## Phase A artifacts\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 4/4 C symbols (`printLine`, `bad`, `good`, `main`) exported by the Rust `.so`; `nm -D` diff **empty**, 0 stubs, 0 unresolved non-libc imports.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 22 rows, mechanically grepped. The C has exactly one explicit check (`if (line != NULL)`), no asserts, no error enums, and discards both `scanf`'s and `printf`'s status — so every \"error\" shows up only as a different branch. There are **no enums in the C source**, so the invalid-enum class is documented as empty (and checked by a test) rather than fabricated.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 41 rows over 7 axes. Features: **none declared**, so 3 cargo invocations × 2 profiles is the whole build cross-product.\n\n## Divergences found and fixed\n\n| | C | untreated Rust | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **SIGPIPE** | killed by signal 13 on a broken-pipe stdout | exit 0 (Rust's runtime sets `SIG_IGN` before `main`) | restore `SIG_DFL` in the executable only |\n| **`scanf` push-back** | pushes exactly **one** character back; a consumed sign is *not* restored | consumed the terminator, so `\"1-9223372036854775809\"` × 2 gave good/**good** vs C's good/**bad** | `ByteSource::push_back` + shared `stdin_state()` in the cdylib |\n| **stdin buffering** | refills in `st_blksize` (4096) chunks and **rewinds a seekable fd at exit** | `std::io::stdin()`'s 8192 buffer, no rewind — `{ ./driver; cat; } < \"1 hello world\"` printed nothing where C prints `\" hello world\"` | `prog::CStdin` + `reposition_if_seekable()` |\n\n## On the undefined behavior\n\n`bad()` reads an uninitialized `char *`. I measured **five different outcomes** from this one `main.c` — `\"\\n\"`, the library's own machine code, `SIGSEGV`, and (in release) `\"string\"`, the pointer a preceding `good()` left in the same slot. I reproduce the outcome every path the *program* takes agrees on (`\"\\n\"`), and deliberately do **not** assert byte-equality where the C's own result changes on a re-link. That last outcome invalidated an earlier row 37 that read branch sequences off stdout — it was rebuilt on stream position, which is fully defined.\n\n## Results\n\n71 tests, green under **all 3 feature invocations × both profiles** (`./verify.sh`, 15 steps). To show the green runs aren't vacuous, `./mutation_check.sh` injects 22 bugs and confirms **22/22 are caught**. That process also exposed two genuine coverage gaps, both closed: whitespace-only inputs can't detect a wrong `isspace()` set (added an exhaustive 256-byte × 3-context classification sweep), and only the *zero-ness* of `x` is observable at all — so value bugs are invisible unless they flip that bit, which is now documented and is why the `k·2³²`/leading-zero rows carry the weight."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1157,"cache_read_input_tokens":305689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1157,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57cca1bc-6361-4d72-86d0-cf8056a9abb0","uuid":"6cdbe1a5-6dc4-4756-8c6a-76895fd7a9f7","timestamp":"2026-08-18T01:56:30.662Z"}
